From d5a8a83d11dcee019c08add6af83501bba41e405 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Willy Tarreau <w@1wt.eu>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 15:18:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] slz: rework FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH as a hash chain with a
 depth limit

The experimental FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH scanned the whole window byte by byte
for each lookup, which made it unusable on large inputs, and stopped at
the second matching candidate despite its name. It now walks a hash chain
built over the whole block on entry, among the first FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH
candidates carrying the same word (0 = all of them), keeping the longest
match and the nearest one on equal lengths. Depth 1 is the last occurrence
of the word, and depth 0 is the longest match within the window, still
parsed greedily. The chain has no collisions and indexes all positions,
including those covered by matches which the hash table never sees (it
only gets the looked up ones plus 3 per match with SLZ_INSERT_SKIPPED).
So depth 1 is the regular lookup without collisions *and* with all
positions indexed, and depth N is an upper bound for N entries per bucket,
not what such buckets would give.
Depth 2 gives exactly the same output as the old scan (index.html at -1:
41.37%, -1 -T: 35.37%). The whole silesia.tar takes 3.9s at depth 0.

Combined with SLZ_STATS, silesia.tar (211947520 bytes), "hash" being the
regular lookup:

